::CW_U05_L05_Q01::A writer has a revised poem, peer feedback notes, and a class online publication space. What is the best next step before publication?{
~A. Ignore the feedback notes.#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered before publication.
=B. Check whether the poem's purpose, format, title, and conventions are publication-ready.#Correct. This completes a responsible publication-readiness review.
~C. Post the oldest draft.#Incorrect. Publication should use the revised, checked version.
~D. Remove the title automatically.#Incorrect. A title often helps online readers enter the piece.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q02::Which statement best defines online collaborative publication?{
~A. Posting a rough draft without review.#Incorrect. That is posting, not publication-ready collaboration.
~B. Letting one peer rewrite the whole piece.#Incorrect. Collaboration should not erase the writer's voice.
~C. Choosing a font before considering meaning.#Incorrect. Visual choices are not enough.
=D. Preparing writing for an online audience through revision, format checks, and shared review roles.#Correct. This matches the lesson's publication workflow.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q03::A writer's purpose is to create a quiet mood. Which feedback decision best protects that purpose?{
=A. Accept feedback that strengthens quiet imagery and reject feedback that makes the ending loud without a purpose.#Correct. This evaluates feedback against the writer's intended effect.
~B. Accept every suggestion automatically.#Incorrect. Not every suggestion supports the writer's purpose.
~C. Reject all feedback automatically.#Incorrect. Some feedback may improve the piece.
~D. Change the piece into a comedy because one peer prefers comedy.#Incorrect. This replaces the writer's purpose.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q04::Which collaborative role is responsible for checking whether the piece is appropriate for the intended online class space?{
~A. Someone who only counts words.#Incorrect. Word count alone does not determine appropriateness.
~B. Someone who changes the writer's theme.#Incorrect. The role should not replace the writer's purpose.
=C. Final reviewer.#Correct. A final reviewer checks readiness, including appropriateness for the audience and space.
~D. Random commenter.#Incorrect. Publication roles should be purposeful.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q05::What is the main reason to include a clear title in an online publication entry?{
=A. It prepares the reader to enter the piece.#Correct. A title can guide the reader's first understanding.
~B. It replaces revision.#Incorrect. A title does not remove the need for revision.
~C. It proves the piece is perfect.#Incorrect. A title alone does not prove readiness.
~D. It lets the writer skip formatting.#Incorrect. Formatting still matters.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q06::Which publication checklist item should happen after receiving peer feedback?{
~A. Delete the piece immediately.#Incorrect. Feedback should be evaluated, not used as a reason to delete automatically.
~B. Use every suggestion without thinking.#Incorrect. Feedback must be checked against purpose.
~C. Ignore feedback because publication is individual only.#Incorrect. This lesson focuses on collaborative publication.
=D. Decide which feedback improves purpose, clarity, format, or reader effect.#Correct. This turns feedback into purposeful revision.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q07::Which item is least publication-ready?{
~A. A revised story with clear paragraphing and a final conventions check.#Incorrect. This sounds publication-ready.
=B. A rough draft copied into the online space with no title, formatting, or review.#Correct. This skips key publication checks.
~C. A poem with purposeful stanza breaks and a brief author note.#Incorrect. These choices can prepare readers well.
~D. A script with character labels and checked stage directions.#Incorrect. These support script publication quality.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q08::What should an author note do?{
~A. Explain every line so readers do not need the creative piece.#Incorrect. That overexplains and replaces the work.
~B. Include private personal information.#Incorrect. Online publication should protect privacy and appropriateness.
=C. Provide helpful context or craft focus without replacing the creative work.#Correct. An author note should prepare the reader while keeping the piece central.
~D. Distract readers from the title.#Incorrect. The author note should support the publication entry.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q09::A peer suggests changing a script into a poem, but the writer's purpose depends on performance and spoken dialogue. What should the writer do?{
~A. Change it into a poem automatically.#Incorrect. This may damage the writer's purpose.
~B. Reject the peer as a person.#Incorrect. Feedback decisions should stay respectful and focused on the work.
~C. Remove all dialogue.#Incorrect. Dialogue is central to the writer's purpose.
=D. Consider the feedback but keep the script form if performance is central to the piece.#Correct. The writer evaluates feedback against purpose and format.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q10::Which choice best shows a format checker doing the role correctly?{
=A. The checker notes that a new speaker in prose dialogue needs a new paragraph.#Correct. This checks format-specific reader clarity.
~B. The checker rewrites the theme without permission.#Incorrect. That goes beyond format checking.
~C. The checker ignores line breaks in a poem.#Incorrect. Line breaks matter in poem format.
~D. The checker only says "good."#Incorrect. This gives no format information.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q11::Which online publication decision is safest and most appropriate?{
~A. Include personal addresses in an author note.#Incorrect. Personal information should not be shared in a class publication space.
~B. Post another student's work without permission.#Incorrect. Collaborative publication must respect authorship.
=C. Use a school-appropriate excerpt and avoid private identifying details.#Correct. This protects audience appropriateness and privacy.
~D. Publish comments that mock a peer.#Incorrect. Publication must remain respectful.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q12::A piece has strong content but many distracting errors. What should happen before online publication?{
~A. Publish it anyway because content is all that matters.#Incorrect. Conventions affect readability.
=B. Complete a final conventions check while preserving the writer's voice.#Correct. Publication readiness includes clarity and conventions.
~C. Change the genre immediately.#Incorrect. The issue is errors, not necessarily genre.
~D. Remove the title only.#Incorrect. That does not address distracting errors.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q13::Which sentence best explains a collaborative publication decision?{
=A. I accepted the suggestion to clarify the title because it helps readers understand the poem's central image.#Correct. This names a decision and explains reader effect.
~B. I changed it because someone told me to.#Incorrect. This does not show reasoning.
~C. I ignored all feedback because collaboration is useless.#Incorrect. This rejects the process.
~D. I changed everything so the piece no longer sounds like mine.#Incorrect. Collaboration should preserve voice and purpose.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q14::Which publication element helps readers know what they are about to read?{
~A. A missing title.#Incorrect. A missing title does not guide readers.
~B. Unchecked spacing.#Incorrect. Unchecked spacing may confuse readers.
~C. Private personal data.#Incorrect. This is not appropriate.
=D. A clear title and, when useful, a brief author note.#Correct. These elements prepare the reader.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q15::What is the problem with accepting every reviewer suggestion?{
~A. It always improves the piece.#Incorrect. Suggestions may conflict with purpose.
~B. It makes format checks unnecessary.#Incorrect. Format still must be checked.
=C. It can weaken the writer's purpose, voice, or intended effect.#Correct. Writers need to evaluate feedback thoughtfully.
~D. It proves the writer is collaborative.#Incorrect. Collaboration is thoughtful, not automatic.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q16::Which sequence best matches publication workflow?{
~A. Post, revise later, ignore readers, remove title.#Incorrect. This reverses readiness checks.
=B. Identify purpose, consider feedback, check format, prepare the reader, complete final review.#Correct. This follows the lesson sequence.
~C. Choose colors, skip feedback, delete draft, submit nothing.#Incorrect. This does not support publication.
~D. Ask one peer to rewrite everything, then publish without checking.#Incorrect. This weakens authorship and readiness.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q17::Which comment best supports a writer preparing for publication?{
~A. Your line breaks are random.#Incorrect. This identifies a problem but does not yet guide revision.
~B. I like it.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~C. Do whatever I would do.#Incorrect. This centers the reviewer instead of the writer.
=D. The final stanza break creates a useful pause; check whether the second stanza also needs that same clarity.#Correct. This gives specific, format-focused publication feedback.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q18::What should a writer do if a piece is not appropriate for the intended online audience?{
=A. Revise, select a different excerpt, or ask the TOR for guidance before submitting it.#Correct. This handles appropriateness responsibly.
~B. Publish it anyway.#Incorrect. Audience appropriateness is required.
~C. Hide the issue with a vague title.#Incorrect. A title does not solve an appropriateness issue.
~D. Blame the audience.#Incorrect. Writers must consider the publication space.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q19::Which statement best describes collaboration in this lesson?{
~A. Collaboration means the loudest reviewer controls the piece.#Incorrect. That does not protect author purpose.
=B. Collaboration means writers and reviewers use roles to improve readiness while respecting the writer's purpose.#Correct. This captures the purpose of collaborative publication.
~C. Collaboration means no one revises.#Incorrect. Revision is part of publication readiness.
~D. Collaboration means format does not matter.#Incorrect. Format matters for online readers.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q20::Why should formatting be checked before online publication?{
~A. Formatting can affect readability, genre expectations, pacing, and reader understanding.#Correct. Format directly shapes the reader's experience.
~B. Formatting is only decoration.#Incorrect. Format can carry meaning and clarity.
~C. Formatting replaces purpose.#Incorrect. Format should support purpose, not replace it.
~D. Formatting should always be random.#Incorrect. Format choices should be purposeful.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q21::Which title decision is strongest?{
=A. Choose a title that connects to the central image or purpose of the piece.#Correct. A purposeful title prepares the reader.
~B. Use no title because titles never matter.#Incorrect. Titles often guide online readers.
~C. Use a misleading title unrelated to the piece.#Incorrect. This can confuse readers.
~D. Use private information as the title.#Incorrect. Publication should protect privacy.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q22::A peer says, "This is ready to publish," but the piece has no clear ending and no format check. What should the writer do?{
~A. Publish immediately because one peer approved it.#Incorrect. One approval does not replace readiness checks.
~B. Ignore the peer rudely.#Incorrect. The writer can respond respectfully.
=C. Thank the peer, then complete the ending and format checks before publication.#Correct. This uses feedback while still checking readiness.
~D. Remove all formatting.#Incorrect. The problem is missing format review, not too much formatting.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q23::Which final review question is most useful?{
~A. Did I post as quickly as possible?#Incorrect. Speed is not the goal.
~B. Did I ignore the reader?#Incorrect. Publication should consider the reader.
~C. Did I erase the writer's voice?#Incorrect. The writer's voice should be protected.
=D. Is the piece revised, formatted, appropriate, and clear for the intended online audience?#Correct. This covers key publication readiness criteria.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q24::Which action respects authorship in collaborative publication?{
~A. Publishing someone else's draft without permission.#Incorrect. This violates authorship.
=B. Giving feedback while leaving final craft decisions to the writer and teacher-guided requirements.#Correct. This supports collaboration and respects the author.
~C. Changing the writer's name.#Incorrect. That does not respect authorship.
~D. Replacing the whole piece with your own.#Incorrect. That erases the author.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q25::Which mastery response best fits Lesson 5?{
=A. My piece is ready for online class publication because I revised from feedback, checked format, prepared the reader with a title, and confirmed audience appropriateness.#Correct. This includes the key publication-readiness components.
~B. My piece is ready because I typed it once.#Incorrect. A typed draft still needs review and readiness checks.
~C. My piece is ready because I ignored feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered.
~D. My piece is ready because I used random spacing.#Incorrect. Random spacing does not prove publication quality.
}
